# tests/crash-probe.floor -- the signal deaths tests/crash-probe.sh EXPECTS.
#
# Every line here is a crash this port has decided not to fix, with the reason
# argued somewhere durable.  The probe fails if the observed set differs from
# this one IN EITHER DIRECTION: a new crash is a regression, and a crash that
# has stopped happening means somebody fixed something and left the floor
# over-stating, which is a place for the next regression to hide.
#
# So fixing a crash requires deleting its line here in the same commit.  That
# is deliberate -- it makes the removal reviewable instead of silent.
#
# FORMAT: "<signal> <label>", matching what the probe prints after "SIGNAL ".
# Blank lines and # comments are ignored.  Generated from a measured run, never
# transcribed: see the regeneration recipe at the bottom.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  The floor used to live in a sentence in CLAUDE.md and
# it rotted -- it read 54 while the truth was 160, because four Wave A2 imports
# added 106 deaths and nothing mechanically checked the number.  A number only
# a human runs is a number that rots.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR GROUPS, AND WHY EACH IS PERMANENT
#
# READ THE COUNTS BEFORE THE LINES.  106 entries are FOUR bugs, and two of them
# account for 104: one mechanism can have fifty spellings, so a long floor is
# not the same thing as a neglected one.  What would be alarming is a new
# PROGRAM, not a new line.
#
# dmesg (51) -- every single-letter option except -i.  Berkeley's, 1981, and
#   unbounded mutual recursion rather than a bad pointer: done() ends
#   `if (wflg) writebuf();' and writebuf() calls done("can't open buffer\n")
#   when it cannot open /usr/adm/msgbuf, so the two call each other until the
#   stack goes.  BUFFER is opened O_WRONLY with no O_CREAT, and the archive
#   ships no /usr/adm at all -- it is runtime state, like /etc/utmp, made when
#   a system is installed rather than shipped in a tarball -- so a VAX
#   recursed identically.
#
#   The count is 51 and not 53 because the switch has exactly one arm that
#   does not set wflg (`case 'i': break;'), and bare `dmesg' never enters the
#   block, which is why the program's documented primary invocation is the one
#   that reports cleanly (`No namelist', exit 1).  src/cmd/dmesg/PORTING.md.
#
# lex (53) -- every invocation including bare.  THREE separate faults, not one,
#   all upstream's on upstream's hardware: see src/cmd/lex/PORTING.md.  The
#   probe feeds every program /dev/null, so all 53 also reach the empty-spec
#   path -- which is why fixing the first fault moved this count by ZERO and
#   the 40 that used to die in warning() now die further along in ctail().
#
# bcd (1) -- bare.  `while ((c=getchar())!='\0' && c!='\n')` never tests EOF,
#   so redirected empty stdin spins and overruns a fixed buffer.  A VAX would
#   have overrun it too.  PLAN.md's S4j table has the argument.
#
# cpio (1) -- `cpio -i`.  The newest member, and the one that took the most
#   measuring.  chgreel()'s fopen("/dev/tty") is unchecked, and V8's /dev/tty
#   is a link to /dev/fd/3, so with no descriptor 3 it returns NULL; the fgets
#   that follows is a getc loop, and getc is `--(p)->_cnt` -- a WRITE to
#   virtual address 0.  V8 binaries are ZMAGIC, whose text is read-only shared,
#   so a VAX took a protection fault there as well.  There is no VAX answer to
#   restore, which is what separates it from the three crashes fixed alongside
#   it.  Third member of the family after pr.c's Ttyin and troff/hc.c's rcf.
#   src/cmd/cpio.PORTING.md has the measurement, both with and without fd 3.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TO REGENERATE after a deliberate change (review the diff -- that is the
# point of the file):
#
#   tests/crash-probe.sh $PWD/rootfs /tmp/pw > /tmp/p.txt
#   { sed -n '1,/^# ENTRIES$/p' tests/crash-probe.floor
#     grep '^SIGNAL ' /tmp/p.txt | sed 's/^SIGNAL //; s/  / /' | sort
#   } > /tmp/floor && mv /tmp/floor tests/crash-probe.floor
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOLERATED, A THIRD CATEGORY THAT CURRENTLY HAS NO MEMBERS
#
# A `?' prefix means "may or may not crash here" -- removed from both sides of
# the comparison, so it can fail neither as new nor as gone.  The count is
# printed every run and entries are named, because an exemption nobody sees is
# how the number in CLAUDE.md rotted in the first place.
#
# IT WAS BUILT FOR ONE ENTRY, `? 11 tar -u', AND DIAGNOSING THAT ENTRY MADE IT
# UNNECESSARY -- which is the argument for not reaching for an escape hatch
# early.  tar -u SIGSEGV'd once on a CI runner and never locally, and was
# recorded first as host-dependent (wrong: the next runner run cleared it three
# times) and then as an unreproduced intermittent (also wrong).  The real cause
# was containment, and it was in the TEST rather than the port: `tar -c' creats
# its default tape /dev/rmt1, creat keys on the parent, $V8ROOT/dev exists, so
# it writes 10240 bytes INTO THE ROOTFS -- and every later tar in the same
# sweep then finds a tape that opens and takes a different path.  Measured:
# tar -u exits 1 without the leftover and 0 with it.  tar is in the probe's
# MUTATES set now, so each of its invocations gets its own rootfs clone.
#
# The mechanism stays because it is verified (both states, on a two-program
# throwaway rootfs) and because the next genuinely flaky crash should not have
# to invent it again.  tests/wavea asserts the set is EXACTLY EMPTY, so adding
# a member is a deliberate edit in two files -- and the bar for adding one is
# now visibly high, since the only candidate so far had a real cause.
#
# ENTRIES
